A||||5||Cliff Pennock||Internet/Network specialists? Need help!||||1||
Z||000000||Cliff Pennock||03-15-2001||07:39 AM||||Ok, very weird problem here. Yesterday afternoon, out of the blue, my internet connection (cable) nearly died on me. My pings shot up to 2000-8000ms and download speeds dropped to a few bytes per second. This happened before in the past and usually it was stuff like broadcast-storms on my segment. So I started my network-monitor to check for this but saw nothing. Then I noticed that even the pingtimes to my cable-modem were incredibly high. This usually means a broken network-card or UTP cable. To check this, I restarted my computer in Linux (I have a dual-boot system) and much to my suprise everything was ok (normal pings, normal download speeds). I restarted in Windows again and got the same virtually non-existing connection as before. I shut down everything except Explorer but nothing changed. Task Info also did not show anything weird (normal processor usage, normal memory usage,no weird programs, etc). I reinstalled everything Network related, and even reinstalled Windows - but no change. I have no virus (for sure). And like I said, everything works perfectly in Linux.<P>This happened once before but that time the problem disappeared after a few hours just as suddenly as it appeared (not after a restart or anything but just during a normal Windows session) but this time it's consistent. If I run my firewall, I see no strange traffic except for one thing: when I start an internet related program (Netscape, ICQ, Ping Plotter) sometimes this program starts sending data at 30KB/s (while the max upload is capped at 8KB/s). If this happenes and I try to shut the program down, it disappears from the screen but not from the task-list and I have to manually shut it down (which usually is followed by a "program is not responding" message).<P>My hardware: Compaq Netelligent 10/100 TX PCI Intel UTP, Surfboard cable modem. Running Windows 98SE.<P>Anyone have any ideas here?||||reg||1
Z||000001||LarryB||03-15-2001||02:24 PM||||OK, you've narrowed down the odds of having a pardware problem quite a bit, so I'd next look at a buggy driver or a badly-behaved or misconfigured application. Are there updated drivers available from either Compaq or Intel for that interface? <P>The other thing I'd try would be to alt-ctl-delete to get the process list and kill off one-at-a-time to try to narrow down the offender.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cliff Pennock||03-15-2001||03:59 PM||||I have the latest driver for the network card. I had also killed every single process (not with Ctrl-Alt-Del, but a program called Task Info which shows <B>all</B> processes running) except the tasks that were absolutely necessary to run Windows. But the problem remained.<P>One thing I forgot to mention is <B>when</B> exactly the problem started: I was running a telnet session, monitoring one of the programs running on my webserver. I left the computer for an hour or so and left the telnet session running. When I returned, I noticed telnet had lost connection due to a problem with my ISP. I closed telnet and left the computer (on) again for an hour or so. When I returned, the connection was back but with the problems described. So the problems didn't occur after a restart or after the installation of something, but in the middle of a Windows session. This problem has got me stunned...||||reg||1
Z||000003||LarryB||03-15-2001||04:08 PM||||Did you get the disconnect because a firewall timed out and disco'd you for inactivity?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cliff Pennock||03-15-2001||04:52 PM||||No, it wasn't the firewall that disconnected me (it was a "burping" router of my ISP).<P>Anyway, small update: my connection is back again. What I did is I unplugged the power of the cable-modem and waited a half hour before connecting it again. I kinda feel like an idiot for not trying that before because that's usually one of the first things I do. The reason I hadn't done that is because everything worked perfectly in Linux. I still have no clue why this has helped. I had reset the cable-modem a few times which didn't help. I think maybe it has something to do with a few left-over threads that never timed out because Windows was still able to reach the cable-modem (and a modem-reset takes about 20 seconds or so, not enough for a time-out). I know not all threads in Windows die automatically when you restart, so maybe this is what happened. I dunno, I still think it's pretty weird.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||03-15-2001||07:56 PM||||Both win9X and NT kill pretty much all threads when you restart the pigs. Unless you do a "short" restart in 9X then only windoze restarts, then it leaves the real mode stuff running. You can't do a short start in NT.<P>What kind of lan card is it?<BR>Is it 10/100? If so you may have to lock it down to 10 and half duplex in the driver settings. <P>If the cable modem is on a 10/100 hub/switch, see if you can set the hub jack speed to 10/half. if you can't do that try stuffing an old 10 only hub in temporary.<P>I've seen allot of stuff, both cards and switches, that has buggy auto sense. It can have really weird results. (We have this problem even on some very expensive switches feeding HP 10 only Jet Direct cards.)||||reg||1
